      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the sum of the greatest common factor (GCF) of 3 and 6 and the least common multiple (LCM) of 3 and 6, we can use the relationship between the GCF and LCM of two numbers. The product of the GCF and LCM of two numbers is equal to the product of the numbers themselves. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ \\text{GCF}(a, b) \\times \\text{LCM}(a, b) = a \\times b \\]\n\nIn this case, \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( b = 6 \\). First, we need to find the GCF of 3 and 6. The GCF of 3 and 6 is 3 because 3 is the largest number that divides both 3 and 6 without leaving a remainder.\n\nNext, we need to find the LCM of 3 and 6. The LCM of 3 and 6 is 6 because 6 is the smallest number that is a multiple of both 3 and 6.\n\nNow, we can use the relationship between the GCF and LCM:\n\n\\[ \\text{GCF}(3, 6) \\times \\text{LCM}(3, 6) = 3 \\times 6 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\times 6 = 18 \\]\n\nSo, the sum of the GCF and LCM is:\n\n\\[ \\text{GCF}(3, 6) + \\text{LCM}(3, 6) = 3 + 6 = 9 \\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given numbers\na = 3\nb = 6\n\n# Calculate the greatest common factor (GCF)\ngcf = math.gcd(a, b)\n\n# Calculate the least common multiple (LCM)\nlcm = abs(a * b) // gcf\n\n# Calculate the sum of GCF and LCM\nresult = gcf + lcm\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe sum of the greatest common factor of 3 and 6 and the least common multiple of 3 and 6 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
